d559fbabd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc516240-edf6-50de-a972-2d32bd44c276" "answer-e9fce1b0-8710-5564-a541-83b6838d2cc1" "answer-e60983fa-e6a9-5ac0-89a9-689d559fbabd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc516240-edf6-50de-a972-2d32bd44c276" :answer/id #uuid "bc516240-edf6-50de-a972-2d32bd44c276" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9fce1b0-8710-5564-a541-83b6838d2cc1" :answer/id #uuid "e9fce1b0-8710-5564-a541-83b6838d2cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e60983fa-e6a9-5ac0-89a9-689d559fbabd" :answer/id #uuid "e60983fa-e6a9-5ac0-89a9-689d559fbabd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368088 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{7{t}^{2}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & =7L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{4}\\} & =\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}\\}=\\frac{24}{(s-1)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =7L\\{{t}^{2}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{24}{(s-1)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c46cfffe-18b2-5eac-8e1f-91eedc4c8269" :activity/id #uuid "c46cfffe-18b2-5eac-8e1f-91eedc4c8269" :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050615 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-f579c56e-583e-5d0a-8431-cb59e1d5eecc" "step-4352ef4f-23b6-5c61-ac25-56e4098a43c6" "step-34e3daa2-8ba3-59ec-8c00-c52a7eef4a03"] :activity/first-step "step-f579c56e-583e-5d0a-8431-cb59e1d5eecc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f579c56e-583e-5d0a-8431-cb59e1d5eecc" :step/id #uuid "f579c56e-583e-5d0a-8431-cb59e1d5eecc" :step/content "question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :step/next "step-4352ef4f-23b6-5c61-ac25-56e4098a43c6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4352ef4f-23b6-5c61-ac25-56e4098a43c6" :step/id #uuid "4352ef4f-23b6-5c61-ac25-56e4098a43c6" :step/content "question-bf25d1c4-52d6-5c8a-89b9-2f0b3253eb28" :step/next "step-34e3daa2-8ba3-59ec-8c00-c52a7eef4a03" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-34e3daa2-8ba3-59ec-8c00-c52a7eef4a03" :step/id #uuid "34e3daa2-8ba3-59ec-8c00-c52a7eef4a03" :step/content 17592186368088 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :question/id #uuid "002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1"] ["question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331789"] ["question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :question/problem "Consider a function $r(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{r(t)\\}=R(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $R(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t=R(s-3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+3.\n$$"] ["question-002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-b2cf6229-1b40-568c-b2ae-1e954c9aa404" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cf6229-1b40-568c-b2ae-1e954c9aa404"] ["answer-74329352-03c2-598b-b3b8-cf7fa3c1748f" :answer/id #uuid "74329352-03c2-598b-b3b8-cf7fa3c1748f"] ["answer-74329352-03c2-598b-b3b8-cf7fa3c1748f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-74329352-03c2-598b-b3b8-cf7fa3c1748f" :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}r(t)"] ["answer-aabb5487-1892-55ca-ba49-3577102d61e1" :answer/id #uuid "aabb5487-1892-55ca-ba49-3577102d61e1"] ["answer-aabb5487-1892-55ca-ba49-3577102d61e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-aabb5487-1892-55ca-ba49-3577102d61e1" :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}r(t)"] ["answer-a81fbb90-ddcd-5c5b-a412-d6fee1a8fc5f" :answer/id #uuid "a81fbb90-ddcd-5c5b-a412-d6fee1a8fc5f"] ["answer-a81fbb90-ddcd-5c5b-a412-d6fee1a8fc5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a81fbb90-ddcd-5c5b-a412-d6fee1a8fc5f" :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}r(t)"] ["answer-42977ec0-6216-5444-bbcb-9b089427c15c" :answer/id #uuid "42977ec0-6216-5444-bbcb-9b089427c15c"] ["answer-42977ec0-6216-5444-bbcb-9b089427c15c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-42977ec0-6216-5444-bbcb-9b089427c15c" :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}r(t)"